You can always use {lo}. You have the option to use {le} when you think=20
that explicitly marking specificity is necessary.

{lo} is generic, but, unlike English, this does not make it wrong to use=20
{lo} when you have specific referents in mind. When you own a specific=20
car, it's also true that you own "any or some" car. {lo} is vague and=20
therefore always applicable. If you don't care about the specificity=20
distinction, then you don't need to use {le}, but if you do, then you can.

